    "char_start": 401769,
    "char_end": 401791,
    "char_count": 22,
    "text": "46. Amendment to Rules",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0986",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 986,
    "page_start": 204,
    "page_end": 204,
    "char_start": 401793,
    "char_end": 401796,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "200",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0987",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 987,
    "page_start": 204,
    "page_end": 204,
    "char_start": 401798,
    "char_end": 403299,
    "char_count": 1501,
    "text": "PREAMBLE\nIn order to seek speedy, affordable, just and reasonable Redressal of Dispute/Differences\nbetween Major Port Trusts and Concessionaire/Contractor arising out of and during the course\nof execution of various contracts, a Society for Affordable Resolution of Disputes - Ports\n(SAROD-PORTS - Ports) has been formed as a Society under Societies Registration Act, 1860\nwith registration ______________. It has been formed by Indian Ports Association and Indian\nPrivate Ports and Terminals Association with founding members as mentioned in the\nMemorandum of Association of SAROD-PORTS\nSAROD-PORTS ARBITRATION\nRULES\nRule: 1 - Scope of Application\n1.1 Where any agreement, submission or reference provides for arbitration at the Society for\nAffordable Resolution of Disputes - Ports (\"SAROD-PORTS\"), or under the Arbitration Rules of\nthe SAROD-PORTS and where the case is a domestic arbitration, the same shall be conducted\nin accordance with the following Rules, or such Rules as amended by the SAROD-PORTS\nwhere the amendments take effect before the commencement of the Arbitration.\n1.2 These rules shall come into effect from the day of approval by Governing Body of SAROD-\nPORTS.\nRule 2 - Definitions\n2.1 These Rules shall be referred to as ''the SAROD-PORTS Arbitration Rules\".\n2.2 In these Rules:\n\"Act\" means the 'Arbitration and Conciliation Act 1996' of India and any statutory modifications\nor re-enactments thereof\n“DOMESTIC ARBITRATION” means arbitration to be conducted under these rules.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0988",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 988,
    "page_start": 205,
    "page_end": 205,
    "char_start": 403301,
    "char_end": 403304,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "201",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0989",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 989,
    "page_start": 205,
    "page_end": 205,
    "char_start": 403306,
    "char_end": 404699,
    "char_count": 1393,
    "text": "\"SAROD-PORTS\" means the Society for Affordable Redressal of Disputes- Ports.\n\"SAROD-PORTS Arbitrator Panel\" means the list of persons admitted to serve as arbitrators\nunder these Rules.\n“IPA” means Indian Ports Association\n\"IPPTA\" means Indian Private Ports and Terminals Association\n\"GOVERNING BODY\" means Governing Body of SAROD-PORTS as defined in Article 9 of\nMemorandum of Association.\n\"PRESIDENT\" means President of Governing Body of SAROD-PORTS as defined in Rules\n&Regulation of SAROD-PORTS.\n“SECRETARY\" means Secretary of SAROD-PORTS as defined in Rules & Regulation of\nSAROD-PORTS.\n\"TRIBUNAL\" means either a Sole Arbitrator or all arbitrators when more than one is appointed.\n“PARTY\" means a party to an arbitration agreement,\n\"E-Arbitration\" means submission of pleadings, defense statement etc. by E-mail and holding\nof proceedings via video conferencing.\nRule 3 - Notice, Calculation of periods of Time\n3.1 For the purposes of these Rules, any notice, including a notification, communication or\nproposal, is deemed to have been received if it is physically delivered to the addressee or if it is\ndelivered at his habitual residence, place of business or mailing address, or, if none of these\ncan be found after making reasonable inquiry, then at the addressee's last-known residence or\nplace of business. Notice shall be deemed to have been received on the day it is so delivered.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0990",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 990,
    "page_start": 206,
    "page_end": 206,
    "char_start": 404701,
    "char_end": 404704,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "202",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0991",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 991,
    "page_start": 206,
    "page_end": 206,
    "char_start": 404706,
    "char_end": 405850,
    "char_count": 1144,
    "text": "3.2 For the purposes of calculating a period of time under these Rules, suchperiod shall begin to\nrun on the day following the day when a notice, notification, communication or proposal is\nreceived. If the last day of such period is an official holiday or a non-business day at the\nresidence or place of business of the addressee, the period is extended until the first business\nday which follows. Gazetted public holidays or non-business days occurring during the running\nof the period of time are included in calculating the period.\n3.3 Without prejudice to the effectiveness of any other form of written communication, written\ncommunication may be made by fax, email or any other means of electronic transmission\neffected to a number, address or site of a party.\n3.4 The transmission is deemed to have been received on the day of transmission.\nRule 4 - Commencement of Arbitration\n4.1 Any party wishing to commence an arbitration under these Rules (\"the Claimant\") shall file\nwith the Secretary and serve on the other party {\"the Respondent\"), a written Notice of\nArbitration (''the Notice of Arbitration\") which shall include the following:",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0992",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 992,
    "page_start": 206,
    "page_end": 206,
    "char_start": 405851,
    "char_end": 406017,
    "char_count": 166,
    "text": "(a) a request that the dispute be referred to arbitration;\n(b) the names, addresses, telephone numbers, fax numbers and email addresses of the parties\nto the dispute;",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0993",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 993,
    "page_start": 206,
    "page_end": 206,
    "char_start": 406018,
    "char_end": 406184,
    "char_count": 166,
    "text": "(c) a reference to the arbitration clause or any separate arbitration agreement that is invoked\nand provide a copy of the arbitration clause or arbitration agreement;",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0994",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 994,
    "page_start": 206,
    "page_end": 206,
    "char_start": 406185,
    "char_end": 406299,
    "char_count": 114,
    "text": "(d) a reference to the contract out of which the dispute arises and provide a copy of the contract\nwhere possible;",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0995",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 995,
    "page_start": 206,
    "page_end": 206,
    "char_start": 406300,
    "char_end": 406392,
    "char_count": 92,
    "text": "(e) a brief statement describing the nature, facts and circumstances leading to the dispute;",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0996",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 996,
    "page_start": 206,
    "page_end": 206,
    "char_start": 406393,
    "char_end": 406519,
    "char_count": 126,
    "text": "(f) the relief or remedy sought, including the amount of claim if quantifiable at the time the\nNotice or Arbitration is filed;",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0997",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 997,
    "page_start": 206,
    "page_end": 206,
    "char_start": 406520,
    "char_end": 406648,
    "char_count": 128,
    "text": "(g) a proposal as to the number of arbitrators (i.e. one or three), if the parties have not\npreviously agreed on the number; and",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0998",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 998,
    "page_start": 206,
    "page_end": 206,
    "char_start": 406649,
    "char_end": 406701,
    "char_count": 52,
    "text": "(h) the name of the Claimant's nominated arbitrator.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-0999",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 999,
    "page_start": 207,
    "page_end": 207,
    "char_start": 406703,
    "char_end": 406706,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "203",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1000",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1000,
    "page_start": 207,
    "page_end": 207,
    "char_start": 406708,
    "char_end": 408439,
    "char_count": 1731,
    "text": "4.2 A filing fee of Rs. 10,000/- (Ten thousand) or any amount decided by Governing Body from\ntime to time is payable at the time of filing the Notice of arbitration.\n4.3 The date of filing of the Notice of Arbitration with the Secretary is the date of\ncommencement of the arbitration for the purpose of these Rules.\nRule 5 - Response by Respondent\n5.1 Within 14 (fourteen) days of receipt of the Notice of Arbitration, the Respondent shall file\nwith the Secretary and serve upon on the Claimant, a Response including\n(a) A confirmation or denial of all or part of the claims;\n(b) Brief statement of the nature and circumstances of any envisaged counterclaims\n(c) A comment in response to any proposals contained in the Notice of Arbitration; and\n(d) The name of the respondent's nominated arbitrator.\n5.2 A filing fee of Rs. 10,000/- or any amount decided by Governing Body from time to time is\npayable at the time of filing the Response.\n5.3 In case parties have objection to the jurisdiction of Arbitral Tribunal, such objection shall be\nraised not later than 15 days of the commencement of Arbitration proceedings failing which it\nwill be deemed that parties have waived their right to objection.\nRule 6 - Filing of Case Statements\n6.1 Within 30 days after the filing of the Notice of Arbitration, the claimant must file with the\nSecretary and serve on the Respondent, a Statement of Claimant's Case along with all\ndocuments to be relied upon by the Claimant.\n6.2 Within 30 days after the service of the statement of Claimant's Case, the Respondent must\nfile with the Secretary and serve on the Claimant, a statement of respondent's defense and\ncounterclaim (if any) along with all documents to be relied upon by the Respondent.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1001",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1001,
    "page_start": 208,
    "page_end": 208,
    "char_start": 408441,
    "char_end": 408444,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "204",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1002",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1002,
    "page_start": 208,
    "page_end": 208,
    "char_start": 408446,
    "char_end": 410089,
    "char_count": 1643,
    "text": "6.3 Within 30 days after the service of the statement of Respondent's defense, if the Claimant\nintends to challenge anything in the statement of Respondent's defense and/or counterclaim,\nthe Claimant must then file with the Secretary and serve on the Respondent, a statement of\nclaimant's reply and if necessary, defense to counterclaim.\n6.4 No further case statements may be filed without the leave of the Tribunal or if a Tribunal has\nnot been appointed, the Secretary.\n6.5 The Tribunal or if a Tribunal has not been appointed, the Secretary, may upon the written\napplication of a party, extend the time limits provided under this Rule,\n6.6 Thy party required to file a case statement must at the same time deposit with the Secretary\nfor eventual transmission to the Tribunal an additional copy or additional copies of the case\nstatement, according to the number of arbitrators constituting or who will constitute the Tribunal.\nRule 7 - Contents of Case Statements\n7.1 The case statements must contain the detailed particulars of the party's claim, defense or\ncounterclaim and must thus contain a comprehensive statement of the facts and contentions of\nlaw supporting the party's position.\n7.2 It must:\na) Set out all items of relief or other remedies sought together with the amount of all\nquantifiable claims and detailed calculations.\nb) State fully its reasons for denying any allegation or statement of the other party.\nc) State fully its own version of events if a party intends to put forward a version of events\ndifferent from that given by the other party.\n7.3 A case statement must be signed by or on behalf of the party making it.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1003",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1003,
    "page_start": 209,
    "page_end": 209,
    "char_start": 410091,
    "char_end": 410094,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "205",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1004",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1004,
    "page_start": 209,
    "page_end": 209,
    "char_start": 410096,
    "char_end": 411691,
    "char_count": 1595,
    "text": "Rule 8 - Default in Filing and Serving Case Statements\n8.1 If the Claimant fails within the time specified under these Rules or as may be fixed by the\nTribunal or by the Secretary, to submit its Statement of Case, the Tribunal or if a Tribunal has\nnot been appointed, the Governing Body may issue an order for the termination of the arbitral\nproceedings or make such other directions as may be appropriate in the circumstances.\n8.2 It the Respondent fails to submit a Statement of Respondent's Defense; the Tribunal may\nnevertheless proceed with the arbitration and make the award.\nRule 9 - Further Written Statements\n9.1 The Tribunal will decide which further written statements, in addition to the case\nstatement(s) already filed, are required from the parties and shall fix the periods of time for\ngiving, filing and serving such statements.\n9.2 All such further statements must be given to the Tribunal, filed with the Secretary and\nserved on the Claimant or Respondent, whichever is applicable.\nRule 10 - SAROD-PORTS to Provide Assistance\nI0.1 At the request of the Tribunal or either party, the Secretary will render such assistance as is\nrequired for the conduct of the arbitration, including arranging for facilities, suitable\naccommodation for sittings of the Tribunal, secretarial assistance or interpretation of these\nrules.\n10.2 Any additional expenses incurred or to be incurred for any such arrangements shall be\nborne by the parties.\nRule 11 - Appointment of Tribunal\n11.1 The disputes shall be decided by a Sole Arbitrator when the total claim of dispute is Rs. 3\nCrores or less.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1005",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1005,
    "page_start": 210,
    "page_end": 210,
    "char_start": 411693,
    "char_end": 411696,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "206",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1006",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1006,
    "page_start": 210,
    "page_end": 210,
    "char_start": 411698,
    "char_end": 413412,
    "char_count": 1714,
    "text": "11.2 In all cases of disputes claimed for more than Rs. 3 Crores, the tribunal shall consist of odd\nnumber of Arbitrators to be nominated by the parties. The Presiding Arbitrator shall be\nappointed by the Arbitrators nominated by the parties from amongst the panel maintained by\nSAROD-PORTS. For deciding the Presiding Arbitrator, a draw of lots can be carried out from\namongst the names suggested by the Arbitrators nominated by the Parties, the eligibility criteria\nfor empanelment of Arbitrators will be decided by the Governing Body.\n11.3 If a Sole Arbitrator is to be appointed, the Governing Body will appoint the Arbitrator within\n21 days from the date the Respondent's Statement of Defense and Counterclaim (if any) is filed\nor falls due, whichever is earlier. The Governing Body will appoint the Arbitrator from the panel\nof Arbitrators by draw of lots,\n11.4 An Arbitrator/Presiding Arbitrator to be appointed under these Rules shall be a person on\nthe SAROD-PORTS Arbitration Panel as at the date of the appointment,\n11.5 In the event of any party failing to appoint Arbitrator within 30 days of receipt of the notice\nof Arbitration, the Governing Body shall appoint the Arbitrator or Presiding Arbitrator as the case\nmay be by a draw of lots.\nRule 12 - Multiparty appointment of the Tribunal\n12.1 If there are more than 2 parties in the arbitration, the parties shall agree on the procedure\nfor appointing the Tribunal within 21 days of the receipt of the Notice of Arbitration.\n12.2 If the parties are unable to do so, upon the lapse of the 21-day time period mentioned\nherein, the Tribunal shall be appointed by the Governing Body as soon as practicable.\nRule 13 - Appointment of Substitute Arbitrator",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1007",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1007,
    "page_start": 210,
    "page_end": 210,
    "char_start": 413413,
    "char_end": 413668,
    "char_count": 255,
    "text": "In the event of the death or resignation of any of the arbitrators, a substitute arbitrator must be\nappointed by the same procedure as in Rule 11 by which the arbitrator concerned was\nappointed, failing which, the Governing Body will make the appointment.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1008",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1008,
    "page_start": 211,
    "page_end": 211,
    "char_start": 413670,
    "char_end": 413673,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "207",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1009",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1009,
    "page_start": 211,
    "page_end": 211,
    "char_start": 413675,
    "char_end": 415269,
    "char_count": 1594,
    "text": "Rule 14 - Independence and Impartiality of the Tribunal\n14.1 The Tribunal conducting arbitration under these Rules shall be and remain at all times\nindependent and impartial and shall not act as advocate for any party.\n14.2 A prospective arbitrator shall disclose to those who approach him in connection with his\npossible appointment, any circumstances likely to give rise to justifiable doubts as to his\nimpartiality or independence.\n14.3 An arbitrator, once nominated or appointed, shall disclose any such circumstance referred\nto in Rule 14.2 to the Secretary and/ or to all parties.\nRule 15 - Code of Ethics for Arbitrators\nAn Arbitrator is a fountain of justice and emblem of equity, fairness and good conscience.\nTherefore he/she is expected to exhibit a noble conduct. The code of conduct prescribed by the\nGoverning Body has to be adopted.\nAppointment\n15.1 A prospective arbitrator shall accept an appointment only if he is fully satisfied that he is\nable to discharge his duties without bias, he has an adequate knowledge of the language of the\narbitration, and he is able to give to the arbitration the time and attention which the parties are\nreasonably entitled to expect,\n15 .2 In this code, the masculine includes the feminine.\nDisclosure\n15.3 A prospective arbitrator shall disclose all facts or circumstances that may give rise to\njustifiable doubts as to his impartiality or independence, such duty to continue thorough out the\narbitral proceedings with regard to new facts and circumstances, in terms of the Arbitration\nand Conciliation Act, 1996 as amended from time to time.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1010",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1010,
    "page_start": 212,
    "page_end": 212,
    "char_start": 415271,
    "char_end": 415274,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "208",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1011",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1011,
    "page_start": 212,
    "page_end": 212,
    "char_start": 415276,
    "char_end": 415398,
    "char_count": 122,
    "text": "15.4 A prospective arbitrator shall disclose to the Secretary and any party who approaches him\nfor a possible appointment:",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1012",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1012,
    "page_start": 212,
    "page_end": 212,
    "char_start": 415399,
    "char_end": 415647,
    "char_count": 248,
    "text": "(a) Any past or present close personal relationship or business relationship, whether direct or\nindirect, with any party to the dispute, or any representative of a party, or any person known\nto be a potentially important witness in the arbitration;",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1013",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1013,
    "page_start": 212,
    "page_end": 212,
    "char_start": 415648,
    "char_end": 417256,
    "char_count": 1608,
    "text": "(b) The extent of any prior knowledge he may have of the dispute.\nBias\n15.5 The criteria for assessing questions relating to bias are impartiality and independence.\nPartiality arises when an arbitrator favors one of the parties or where he is prejudiced in relation\nto the subject matter of the dispute. Dependence arises from relationships between an arbitrator\nand one of the parties, or with someone closely connected with one of the parties.\n15.6 Any close personal relationship or current direct or indirect business relationship between\nan arbitrator and a party, or any representative of a party, or with a person who is known to be a\npotentially important witness, will normally give rise to justifiable doubts as to a prospective\narbitrator's impartiality or independence. Past business relationships will only give rise to\njustifiable doubts if they are of such magnitude or nature as to be likely to affect a prospective\narbitrator's judgment. He should decline to accept an appointment in such circumstances unless\nthe parties agree in writing that he may proceed.\nCommunications\n15.7 Before accepting an appointment, an arbitrator may only enquire as to the general nature\nof the dispute, the names of the parties and the expected time period required for the arbitration.\n15.8 No arbitrator shall Communicate with any of the parties or their Counsel until after the\nSecretary gives notice of the formation of the Tribunal to the parties.\n15.9 Throughout the arbitral proceedings, an arbitrator shall avoid any unilateral\ncommunications regarding the case with any party, or its representatives.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1014",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1014,
    "page_start": 213,
    "page_end": 213,
    "char_start": 417258,
    "char_end": 417261,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "209",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1015",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1015,
    "page_start": 213,
    "page_end": 213,
    "char_start": 417263,
    "char_end": 418778,
    "char_count": 1515,
    "text": "Fees\n15.10 In accepting an appointment, an arbitrator agrees to the remuneration asprescribed in the\nrules of SAROD-PORTS, and he shall make no unilateral arrangements with any of the parties\nor their Counsel for any additional fees or expenses without the agreement of all the parties and\nthe consent of the Secretary of SAROD-PORTS.\nConduct\n15.11 Once the arbitration proceedings commence, the arbitrator shall acquaint himself with all\nthe facts and arguments presented and all discussions relative to the proceedings so that he\nmay properly understand the dispute.\nConfidentiality\n15.12 The arbitration proceedings shall remain confidential. An arbitratoris in a relationship of\ntrust to the parties and should not, at any time, use confidential information acquired during the\ncourse of the proceedings to gain personal advantage or advantage for others, or to affect\nadversely the interest of another.\n15.13 This Code is not intended to provide grounds for the setting aside of any award.\nRule 16 - Challenge of Arbitrators\n16.1 An arbitrator may be challenged if there are circumstances that give rise to justifiable\ndoubts as to his impartially or independence and also if he or she has committed any\nmisconduct\n16.2 An arbitrator may also be challenged if he does not possess thequalifications required by\nthe agreement of the parties,\n16.3 A party may challenge an arbitrator appointed on its nomination or with its agreement only\nfor reasons of which it becomes aware after the appointment has been made.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1016",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1016,
    "page_start": 214,
    "page_end": 214,
    "char_start": 418780,
    "char_end": 418783,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "210",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1017",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1017,
    "page_start": 214,
    "page_end": 214,
    "char_start": 418785,
    "char_end": 420524,
    "char_count": 1739,
    "text": "16.4 A party who intends to challenge an arbitrator shall file with the Secretary and serve on the\nother party or all other parties, whichever is applicable, a Notice of Challenge.\n16.5 The Notice of challenge must be filed and served within 14 days from the appointment of\nthe arbitrator or within 14 days after the circumstances mentioned in Rule 15.1 became known\nto that party.\n16.6 The Notice of Challenge must state the reasons for the challenge.\n16. 7 The arbitration shall be suspended until the challenge is resolved or decided upon.\n16.8 When an arbitrator has been challenged by one party, the other party may agree to the\nchallenge. The arbitrator may also, after the challenge, withdraw from his office. However, it is\nnot implied in either case that there has been an acceptance of the validity of the grounds for\nthe challenge. In both cases, the procedure provided in Rule 11 read with Rule 13, shall be\nused for the appointment of a substitute arbitrator.\nRule 17 - Decision on Challenge\n17.1 If the other party does not agree to the challenge and the arbitrator does not withdraw, the\ndecision on the challenge will be made by the Governing Body.\n17.2 If the Governing Body sustains the challenge, a substitute arbitrator shall be appointed or\nchosen pursuant to the procedure applicable to the appointment of an arbitrator as provided in\nRule 11 read with Rule 13. If the Governing Body dismisses the challenge, the arbitrator shall\ncontinue with the arbitration.\nRule 18 - Removal of the Tribunal\n18.1 The Governing Body may on the application of a party remove an arbitrator:\n(a) Who is physically or mentally incapable of conducting the proceedings or where there are\njustifiable doubts as to his ability to do so; or",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1018",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1018,
    "page_start": 215,
    "page_end": 215,
    "char_start": 420526,
    "char_end": 420529,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "211",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1019",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1019,
    "page_start": 215,
    "page_end": 215,
    "char_start": 420531,
    "char_end": 420641,
    "char_count": 110,
    "text": "(b) Who has refused or failed to use all reasonable dispatch in conducting the arbitration or\nmaking an award.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1020",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1020,
    "page_start": 215,
    "page_end": 215,
    "char_start": 420642,
    "char_end": 422399,
    "char_count": 1757,
    "text": "(c) Who has continuously absented from attending the proceedings for more than 3 sitting\nwithout prior permission of Presiding Arbitrator/Governing Body of SAROD-PORTS.\n18.2 The arbitrator(s) concerned is entitled to appear and be heard at the hearingof the\napplication to remove him.\n18.3 Upon the removal of the arbitrator, a substitute arbitrator shall beappointed in accordance\nwith Rule 11 read with Rule 13.\n18.4 The Governing Body's decision on the application is final and is notsubject to appeal or\nreview.\nRule 19 - Re-hearing in the Event of Replacement of the Tribunal\nIf the sole or presiding Arbitrator is replaced, there shall be a re-hearing. If any other arbitrator is\nreplaced, such re-hearing may take place at the discretion of the Tribunal.\nRule 20 - Jurisdiction of the Tribunal\n20.1 The Tribunal shall have the power to rule on its own jurisdiction, including any objection\nwith respect to the existence, termination or validity of the arbitration agreement. For that\npurpose, an arbitration agreement which forms part of a contract shall be treated as an\nagreement independent of the other terms of the contract. A decision by the Tribunal that the\ncontract is null and void shall not entail ipso jure the invalidity of the arbitration agreement.\n20.2 The plea that the Tribunal does not have jurisdiction shall be raised not later than in the\nStatement of Defense. A plea that the Tribunal is exceeding the scope of its authority shall be\nraised promptly after the Tribunal has indicated its intention to decide on the matter alleged to\nbe beyond the scope of its authority. [neither case the Tribunal may nevertheless admit a late\nplea under this Rule if it considers the delay justified. A party is not precluded from raising such",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1021",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1021,
    "page_start": 215,
    "page_end": 215,
    "char_start": 422400,
    "char_end": 422493,
    "char_count": 93,
    "text": "a plea by the fact that he has nominated or participated in the appointment of an arbitrator.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1022",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1022,
    "page_start": 216,
    "page_end": 216,
    "char_start": 422495,
    "char_end": 422498,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "212",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1023",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1023,
    "page_start": 216,
    "page_end": 216,
    "char_start": 422500,
    "char_end": 424284,
    "char_count": 1784,
    "text": "20.3 The Tribunal must rule on an objection that it lacks jurisdiction as a preliminary question\nupon the objection being raised. It may rule on an objection that it exceeds the scope of its\nauthority either as a preliminary question or in an award on the merits, as it deems just and\nconvenient.\n20.4 In addition to the jurisdiction to exercise the powers defined elsewhere in these Rules, the\nTribunal shall have jurisdiction to determine any question of law arising in the arbitration;\nproceed with the arbitration notwithstanding the failure or refusal of any party to comply with\nthese Rules or with the Tribunal's orders or directions, or to attend any meeting or hearing, but\nonly after giving that party written notice that it intends to do so; and to receive and take into\naccount such written or oral evidence as it shall determine to be relevant, whether or not strictly\nadmissible in law.\nRule 21 - Fees of SAROD-PORTS and Arbitral Tribunal\nFee Schedule\nRegistration Fee (Non - Refundable): Rs.10,000/- or any amount fixed by Governing Body from\ntime to time. The Schedule of Fees and allied expenditure shall be decided by Governing Body.\nRule 22 - Transmission of File to the Tribunal\n22.1 The Secretary shall, as soon as practicable transmit to the Tribunal, a file containing the\nNotice of Arbitration, the Response and all case statements.\n22.2 The Tribunal shall as soon as practicable, after consultation with the parties, issue such\norders and/or directions as are necessary for the conduct of the arbitration to conclusion,\nincluding a timetable for steps to be taken in the arbitration and for the hearing of the arbitration.\nRule 23 - Judicial Seat of Arbitration\n23.1 Unless otherwise agreed by the parties, the judicial seat of arbitration shall be New Delhi.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1024",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1024,
    "page_start": 217,
    "page_end": 217,
    "char_start": 424286,
    "char_end": 424289,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "213",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1025",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1025,
    "page_start": 217,
    "page_end": 217,
    "char_start": 424291,
    "char_end": 425787,
    "char_count": 1496,
    "text": "23.2 Notwithstanding Rule 22.1 and 22.2, the Tribunal may, unless otherwise agreed by the\nparties, hold hearings and meetings anywhere convenient, subject to the provisions of Rule\n28.2.\nRule 24 - Language of Arbitration\nThe language of arbitrators shall be English. In case of material existing are in any other\nlanguage, other than English the same has to be translated to English language.\nRule 25 - Conduct of the Proceedings\nThe Tribunal shall have the widest discretion allowed by the Act to ensure the just, expeditious,\neconomical and final determination of the dispute. The proceedings shall be conducted from 10\nAM to 5 PM with a recess of one hour.\nRule 26 - Communication between Parties and the Tribunal\n26.1 Where the Tribunal sends any written communication to one party, it shall send a copy to\nthe other party or parties as the case may be.\n26.2 Where a party sends any written communication (including Statements, expert reports or\nevidentiary documents) to the Tribunal, the same shall be copied to the other party or ail other\nparties, whichever is applicable, and show to the Tribunal that the same has been so copied.\n26.3 The address of the parties for the purpose of all communications during the proceedings\nshall be those set out in the Notice of Arbitration, or as either party may at any time notify the\nTribunal and the other party or parties, whichever is applicable.\n26.4 A copy of correspondence between the parties and the Tribunal shall be sent to the\nSecretary.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1026",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1026,
    "page_start": 218,
    "page_end": 218,
    "char_start": 425789,
    "char_end": 425792,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "214",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1027",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1027,
    "page_start": 218,
    "page_end": 218,
    "char_start": 425794,
    "char_end": 427522,
    "char_count": 1728,
    "text": "Rule 27 - Party Representatives\nAny party may be represented by legal practitioners or any other representatives, subject to\nsuch proof of authority as the Tribunal may require. The names and addresses of such\nrepresentatives must be notified to the other party or parties. In case one party is represented\nby non-legal person, another party will also be represented by non-legal person so as to\nmaintain natural justice.\nRule 28 - Hearings\n28.1 Unless the parties have agreed on documents- only arbitration the tribunal shall hold a\nhearing for the presentation of evidence by witnesses, including expert witnesses, or for oral\nsubmissions.\n28.2 The Tribunal shall fix the date, time and place of any meetings and hearings in the\narbitrations on the first hearing, and complete time table pertaining to all the activities of the\nArbitration e.g. submission of statement of claim, reply, counter claim, reply therein, admission\nand denial of documents, visit/inspection of site if any. The tribunal shall stick to the timetable\nwith or without any deviations unless there are unavoidable circumstances warranting such\ndeviation which will be with the prior permission of the tribunal.\n28.3 Prior to the hearing, the Tribunal may provide the Parties with matters or questions to\nwhich it wishes them to give special consideration.\n28.4 In the event that a party to the proceedings without sufficient cause, fails to appear at a\nhearing of which the notice has been given, the Tribunal may proceed with the arbitration and\nmay make the Award after the party present has submitted evidence to prove its case.\n28.5 All meetings and hearing shall be in private unless the parties agree otherwise.\nRule 29 - Documents Only Arbitration",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1028",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1028,
    "page_start": 218,
    "page_end": 218,
    "char_start": 427523,
    "char_end": 427614,
    "char_count": 91,
    "text": "29.1 The Disputes may be decided without an oral hearing if it is so agreed by the parties.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1029",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1029,
    "page_start": 219,
    "page_end": 219,
    "char_start": 427616,
    "char_end": 427619,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "215",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1030",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1030,
    "page_start": 219,
    "page_end": 219,
    "char_start": 427621,
    "char_end": 429258,
    "char_count": 1637,
    "text": "29.2.1 Where the parties agree to dispense with oral hearing, the Tribunal must be promptly\ninformed by either of the parties, as soon as is practicable. The Tribunal must also be promptly\ninformed it, at a later stage, the parties or either of them intends to apply for an oral hearing.\n29.2.2 Parties may seek discovery of documents if they are not satisfied with existence of\ndocuments annexed with statement of claim, reply and counter claim by giving self-contained\nrequest to the Tribunal justifying the necessity for such documents. Decision of tribunal shall be\nfinal and binding upon the parties.\nRule 30 - Witnesses\n30.1 The Tribunal may require each party to give notice of the names and designations of the\nwitnesses it intends to call and reasons for legal necessity of such witness.\n30.2 No party shall call any expert witness without the leave of the Tribunal.\n30.3 Any witness who gives evidence may be questioned by each party or its representative\nsubject to any rulings made by the Tribunal,\n30.4 A Witness may be required by the Tribunal to testify under oath or affirmation\n30.5 Subject to such order or direction which the Tribunal may make, the testimony of witness\nmay be presented in written form, either as signed statements or by duly sworn or affirmed\naffidavits,\n30.6 Any party may require a witness to attend an oral examination at a hearing. If the witness\nfails to attend, the Tribunal may place such weight on the written testimony as it thinks fit, or\nmay exclude it altogether,\n30.7 The Tribunal shall determine the admissibility, relevance, materiality and weight of the\nevidence given by any witness.",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1031",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1031,
    "page_start": 220,
    "page_end": 220,
    "char_start": 429260,
    "char_end": 429263,
    "char_count": 3,
    "text": "216",
    "is_boilerplate": true,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1032",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1032,
    "page_start": 220,
    "page_end": 220,
    "char_start": 429265,
    "char_end": 430869,
    "char_count": 1604,
    "text": "Rule 31 - Experts Appointed by the Tribunal\n31.1 Unless otherwise agreed by the parties, the Tribunal may:\n(a) appoint one or more experts to report the Tribunal on specific issues;\n(b) require a party to give any such expert any relevant information or to produce, or to provide\naccess to, any relevant documents, goods or property for inspection by the expert.\n31.2 Unless otherwise agreed by the parties, if a party so requests or if the Tribunal deem it fit,\nthe expert shall, after delivery of his written or oral report, participate in an oral hearing, at which\nthe parties may question him and present expert witnesses in order to testify on the points at\nissue.\n31.3 Rule 30.2 shall not apply to an assessor appointed by agreement of the parties, or to an\nexpert appointed by the Tribunal to advise solely in relation to procedural matters.\nRule 32 - Rules applicable to substance of dispute - Where the place of arbitration\nis situated in India\n32.1 In an arbitration, the arbitral tribunal shall decide the dispute submitted to arbitration in\naccordance with the substantive law for the time being in force in India;\nRule 33 - Closure of Hearing\n33.1 The Tribunal may inquire of the parties if they have any further proof to offer or witnesses\nto be heard or submission to make and, if there are none, declare the hearing closed.\n33.2 The Tribunal may also, in view of exceptional circumstance, reopen the hearings at any\ntime before the award is made.\nRule 34 - Additional Powers of the Tribunal\n34.1 In addition to the powers conferred by the Act, the Tribunal shall also have the power to: -",
    "is_boilerplate": false,
    "is_superseded": false
  },
  {
    "paragraph_id": "PAR-DOC076-1033",
    "doc_id": "DOC-076",
    "paragraph_index": 1033,
    "page_start": 221,
